# Line endings.
#
# This project is authored on Windows and runs on Linux, which makes CRLF a real
# hazard rather than a style preference:
#
#   * tools/verify.sh with CRLF fails on Linux with "bad interpreter: no such
#     file or directory" -- an error that points at the shell, not at the line
#     endings, and reliably wastes twenty minutes.
#   * schema.sql and server.cfg are read by tools that mostly tolerate CRLF, but
#     "mostly" is doing a lot of work there.
#
# So: normalise everything to LF in the repository. Git still checks out CRLF
# locally for the file types where Windows tooling expects it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the files where it matters most.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.lua   text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
# Game data. The GAME parses these, not us, and its parser is not ours to
# forgive a stray CR with -- so pin them the same way the shell scripts are
# pinned rather than leaving them to `text=auto`.
*.meta  text eol=lf

# Binary -- never touch these.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
# Audio, and it arrived with the vendored pma-voice (ui/mic_click_*.ogg). Git's
# `text=auto` heuristic would almost certainly classify these as binary on its
# own, but "almost certainly" is not a property to hang a sound file on: a
# single CRLF substitution inside an Ogg container is silent corruption that no
# gate here would notice and only a playtest would.
*.ogg   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ytd   binary
*.ydr   binary
*.yft   binary
*.ymap  binary
*.ybn   binary

# Generated. Collapses diff noise on the built bundle, which changes wholesale
# on every build and is never reviewed line by line.
resources/[fivem-royale]/br_ui/ui/**  linguist-generated=true -diff
ui-src/package-lock.json              linguist-generated=true -diff
